HUB.talks = {
  "1-1": {"src": "talks/audio/1-1.mp3?v=74101e51", "dur": 238.76, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Hey, welcome in. Get comfortable, because today we're starting at the very beginning: what accounting actually is, and why it matters to you.", 0.0], ["Most people picture an accountant hunched over a spreadsheet, typing numbers into little boxes. And sure, there's some of that. But really, accounting is a language. It's a set of concepts and techniques for measuring and reporting financial information about an economic unit. Usually that unit is one business, and its money is kept separate from the personal money of its owners.", 10.52], ["So who's listening when a business talks in this language? A lot of people. Managers use it to run the place. Owners and investors want to know if their money is growing. Banks and other creditors want to know if they'll be paid back. Government agencies use it for taxes and regulation. Analysts compare companies, and employees wonder if their jobs, and their raises, are safe. Each one is asking a question about their own stake in the business.", 33.36], ["Now here's the key insight. Managers sit inside the company. They can ask for any report they want, in any format. Everyone else sits outside, and they only get what the company chooses to publish. That one difference splits accounting into two big branches.", 62.71], ["Financial accounting serves the outsiders. Because outsiders can't see the details, and they want to compare one company with another, everyone has to play by the same rules. Those rules are called generally accepted accounting principles, or GAAP. In the United States, the Financial Accounting Standards Board, the FASB, writes them. Internationally, the IASB issues International Financial Reporting Standards. The reports that come out are general purpose: they're not tailored to any one reader, they're meant to be neutral, relevant, and faithful to what really happened.", 78.27], ["Managerial accounting serves the insiders, and management sets the rules. If a manager thinks some development costs are really a long-term investment, they can leave them out of an internal profit report. That's their call. The reports should still be logical, but there's no rulebook they have to follow.", 113.67], ["Quick word on technology. The tedious bookkeeping, recording every transaction by hand, is now mostly done by software, and more and more by artificial intelligence. That frees accountants to do the interesting part: thinking, judging, and helping people make decisions.", 133.09], ["And judgment is a big deal, because accounting numbers are not as exact as they look. Here's an example. Lakeside Motors sells a car for thirty thousand dollars. The car cost them twenty-four thousand, and it comes with a three-year warranty. So how much profit did they make? Well, nobody will know the true warranty cost for three years. But nobody wants to wait three years for a profit number, because by then it's useless. So the dealer makes a reasonable estimate, say nine hundred dollars, and reports a profit of about five thousand one hundred. That's accounting: timely information built on careful estimates.", 150.5], ["One last idea. What's something worth? Many items, like land, are kept on the books at historical cost, the price actually paid. That number is reliable, because you can check the receipt. The alternative is fair value: what the item is worth today. That's more relevant, but it's an opinion, so it's less reliable. Standard setters keep moving the line between the two, a little more toward fair value over time.", 190.05], ["So, to wrap up. Accounting measures and reports a business's finances for lots of different users. Financial accounting follows GAAP for outsiders. Managerial accounting follows management's own rules for insiders. And behind every number is some judgment. Keep that in mind, and the rest of this course will make a lot more sense. See you in the next lesson.", 216.11]]},
  "1-2": {"src": "talks/audio/1-2.mp3?v=9fbc0b5b", "dur": 228.75, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. Last time we talked about what accounting is. Today, let's talk about who does it, and where.", 0.0], ["Saying you want to be an accountant is a lot like saying you want to be a doctor. There are specialties, and they're pretty different from each other. Accountants work in three broad places: public accounting, private accounting, and government.", 8.13], ["Let's start with public accounting. These are firms that serve the general public. A business, a nonprofit, or just a regular person hires an accounting firm the same way they'd hire a law firm. Public accountants do three main kinds of work.", 22.95], ["First, auditing. An auditor examines the transactions and systems behind a company's financial reports, and then gives an independent opinion on whether those statements are presented fairly. Think of it like a home inspection before you buy a house. You don't just trust the seller; you want someone independent to look.", 40.07], ["Second, tax. Preparing and filing returns, sure, but also advising clients before they make a decision, so they understand the tax consequences ahead of time.", 63.22], ["Third, consulting. That can be almost anything, from designing an information system to studying how a company makes its products.", 72.46], ["Public accounting usually requires a license. In the United States, each state licenses Certified Public Accountants, or CPAs. Other countries have similar titles, like Chartered Accountant. The license tells the public: this person met real standards for education, exams, and experience, and they've agreed to follow a code of ethics.", 82.78], ["Next, private accounting, sometimes called industry accounting. These accountants work for one employer, a business big or small, or a not-for-profit like a hospital, university, or charity. They figure out what things cost, build budgets, analyze investments, handle taxes and finance. Some are internal auditors. And here's a distinction worth remembering. An internal auditor is an employee looking inward, checking their own company's controls, protecting its resources. An external auditor is an independent outsider, reporting to the public. Same word, \"auditor,\" very different seat at the table.", 107.42], ["Then there's government. Every level, local, state, and national, needs accountants. At the federal level you'll find them at the IRS, collecting taxes and examining returns. At the GAO, the watchdog that audits how federal money is spent. At the SEC, which oversees financial reporting by public companies. And even at the FBI, where forensic accountants follow the money in fraud cases.", 146.76], ["Now, the most important part of today. Ethics. Investors and lenders make big decisions based on financial statements. If those statements aren't honest, the whole system falls apart. So the profession builds in several safeguards. There's a code of ethics every licensed accountant must follow. There's the independent audit. There's government oversight, like the SEC. And, there's the ever-vigilant lawyer, because people who are hurt by bad reporting can sue.", 172.92], ["So here's the takeaway. Accountants work in public firms, private companies, and government. CPAs are licensed to serve the public, auditors give independent opinions, and through all of it, integrity is the whole job. People trust accountants with their money. That trust has to be earned every single day. See you next time.", 207.23]]},
  "1-3": {"src": "talks/audio/1-3.mp3?v=fe9ced08", "dur": 219.48, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. Today we're looking at the single most important idea in this entire course. One equation. Every accounting system in the world, from a lemonade stand to a giant corporation, is built on it.", 0.0], ["It goes back more than five hundred years, to a Renaissance monk named Luca Pacioli. He came up with a way for merchants to track whether their trading ventures were working. And his method is still the foundation of the most sophisticated software today.", 14.36], ["Here's the idea. A business is a collection of stuff it owns, plus the claims against that stuff. So: Assets equal Liabilities plus Owners' Equity.", 29.95], ["Let's unpack those three words. Assets are the resources a business controls, things expected to bring future benefits. Cash, inventory, equipment, land. Liabilities are what the business owes to others: loans, bills from suppliers, any existing obligation to pay or to do something. And owners' equity is what's left over for the owners.", 41.64], ["Because it's an equation, you can rearrange it. Assets minus Liabilities equals Owners' Equity. In plain English: if the business paid off every creditor, whatever remains belongs to the owners. That's why equity is also called net assets.", 65.85], ["Think of a house. Say it's worth three hundred thousand, and you owe two hundred thousand on the mortgage. Your equity is one hundred thousand. Same logic, exactly.", 82.48], ["Now, who the \"owners\" are depends on the type of business. A sole proprietorship has one owner, so one capital account. A partnership has one capital account for each partner. A corporation is owned by stockholders, and its equity comes in two pieces. Capital stock is what stockholders put in when they bought shares. Retained earnings are profits the company earned and kept, instead of paying them out.", 93.06], ["Retained earnings go up when the company earns net income. They go down with net losses, and with dividends. Dividends are payments to shareholders, a return on their investment. Once you pay earnings out, they're no longer retained. Makes sense, right?", 119.68], ["Now let's put the equation on paper. That's the balance sheet, also called the statement of financial position. It lists the assets, liabilities, and equity at one moment in time, like a snapshot. Take Edelweiss Corporation. It has assets of eight hundred ninety-five thousand dollars. It owes creditors one hundred seventy-five thousand. So stockholders' equity is seven hundred twenty thousand. And that equity came from two places: one hundred twenty thousand that stockholders invested, and six hundred thousand the company earned and kept over the years.", 136.8], ["Here's a trap, though. Is Edelweiss worth seven hundred twenty thousand dollars? No! Lots of assets are recorded at what they cost, not what they're worth today. Their land cost a hundred twenty-five thousand, but it could be worth much more now. And some valuable things aren't on the balance sheet at all, like a strong brand or a trade secret. Some risks, like a pending lawsuit, may not show up either.", 174.03], ["So, the takeaway. Assets equal liabilities plus owners' equity, always. Equity is what's left for the owners. And the balance sheet shows that equation at a single point in time. Just remember, it's not a price tag for the whole business. Nice work today.", 202.66]]},
  "1-4": {"src": "talks/audio/1-4.mp3?v=c246fb92", "dur": 228.05, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. Last time we learned the big equation: assets equal liabilities plus owners' equity. Today we're going to put it to work, and watch what happens when a business actually does something.", 0.0], ["Every time a business does something with money, we call it a transaction. Buying, selling, borrowing, paying. And each transaction changes the numbers. But here's the beautiful part. If it's recorded properly, a transaction never breaks the equation. Ever.", 13.98], ["There are really only two ways that happens. Either one asset goes up and another goes down by the same amount, so the totals don't move. Or both sides of the equation go up, or both go down, together.", 31.06], ["Let's watch it with Edelweiss Corporation, the company from last time. They start with twenty-five thousand dollars of cash and fifty thousand of accounts receivable. That's money customers owe them.", 45.42], ["Transaction A. A customer pays ten thousand dollars that they already owed. Cash goes up ten thousand. Accounts receivable goes down ten thousand. Notice, this is not a new sale. The company just traded one asset, an I-O-U, for another asset, cash. Total assets? Unchanged.", 58.42], ["Transaction B. Edelweiss buys thirty thousand dollars of equipment and borrows the money to pay for it. Equipment goes up thirty thousand. Loans payable, a liability, also goes up thirty thousand. Both sides rise together, so the equation still balances.", 77.82], ["Now, Transactions C and D are where students usually get tripped up, so let's slow down.", 95.16], ["Transaction C. Edelweiss does five thousand dollars of work for a customer, who promises to pay later. Accounts receivable goes up five thousand. Easy. But what's the other side? Here's the logic. Doing work for customers is revenue. Revenue increases income. And income, when it's kept in the business, becomes retained earnings, which is part of equity. So retained earnings goes up five thousand. Assets up five thousand, equity up five thousand. Balanced.", 101.34], ["Transaction D. Edelweiss pays three thousand dollars of expenses, maybe rent or utilities. Cash goes down three thousand. And expenses reduce income, which reduces retained earnings. So equity goes down three thousand too. Still balanced.", 133.25], ["See the pattern? Revenue pushes retained earnings up. Expenses pull it down.", 150.51], ["Let's define those carefully. Revenue is the benefit a business gets from providing goods or services to customers. Expenses are the costs of doing business, the outflows it takes to produce those goods and services.", 156.26], ["And one more thing. In everyday conversation, people say \"revenue\" and \"income\" like they mean the same thing. In accounting, they don't. Revenue is the total amount generated by the business. It's the top line of the income statement. Some countries call it turnover. Income is what's left after you subtract all the expenses. That's the bottom line. So: revenues minus expenses equals income.", 169.85], ["A coffee shop that sells a million dollars of coffee has a million in revenue. But if it spent nine hundred fifty thousand running the place, its income is only fifty thousand. Big difference.", 197.23], ["So, to recap. Every transaction changes at least two things, and the equation always stays in balance. Revenue raises retained earnings, expenses lower it. And revenue is the top line, income is the bottom line. You've got this. See you next lesson.", 210.9]]},
  "1-5": {"src": "talks/audio/1-5.mp3?v=7dd501b7", "dur": 220.6, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. Imagine someone offers you a chance to buy stock in a company. What would you want to know first? Is it profitable? Does it have cash? Does it owe a lot of money? Those are the questions financial statements are built to answer.", 0.0], ["Some questions can't be answered with numbers, of course. Is management any good? Is a competitor about to crush them? For that, investors also read the notes to the statements, company websites, regulatory filings, and the financial press. But don't expect special private tips. Securities laws strictly limit companies from giving inside information to just one investor.", 15.37], ["Financial accounting talks through four standard reports. Let's walk through them using Quartz Corporation.", 38.95], ["First, the income statement. It answers: how much did we earn? Quartz had revenues of six hundred sixty thousand dollars and expenses of five hundred eighty thousand, so net income was eighty thousand. Notice its heading says \"For the Year Ended December thirty-first.\" It covers a period of time.", 47.63], ["Second, the statement of retained earnings. It's a bridge. Quartz started the year with two hundred twenty thousand of retained earnings. Add the eighty thousand of net income. Subtract thirty thousand of dividends. And it ends the year with two hundred seventy thousand.", 65.55], ["Third, the balance sheet. This one's a snapshot, at one moment: December thirty-first. Quartz has eight hundred thousand of assets, two hundred eighty thousand of liabilities, and five hundred twenty thousand of stockholders' equity. And look inside that equity. There's the two hundred seventy thousand of retained earnings, carried right over from the second statement.", 84.03], ["Fourth, the statement of cash flows. It explains why cash changed, in three groups. Operating activities, the day-to-day business, brought in thirty-five thousand. Investing activities used sixty thousand, to buy equipment. Financing activities brought in twenty thousand: a fifty thousand dollar loan, minus thirty thousand paid out as dividends. Net result, cash dropped five thousand, from sixty-five thousand down to sixty thousand.", 107.53], ["Wait a second, though. Quartz earned eighty thousand, but operations only brought in thirty-five thousand of cash. How? Because income and cash don't always arrive at the same time. If you do work on credit, you've earned the income, but the cash hasn't come in yet. Over time, those differences tend to even out.", 133.93], ["Two more traps. Dividends are not an expense. They're a distribution of income to the owners, so they show up on the retained earnings statement, not the income statement. And borrowing money brings in cash, but it isn't income. It's financing.", 157.92], ["Now here's my favorite part. These statements articulate, which means they lock together. Net income from the income statement flows into retained earnings. Ending retained earnings flows onto the balance sheet. And when you drop it in, the balance sheet balances. Exactly. It feels almost like magic, and it's why Pacioli's five-hundred-year-old system is still with us.", 172.77], ["So, the takeaways. Four statements: income statement, retained earnings, balance sheet, and cash flows. The balance sheet is a moment in time; the others cover a period. Cash is not the same as income. And the statements all fit together like puzzle pieces. That wraps up chapter one. Really nice work.", 200.93]]},
  "2-1": {"src": "talks/audio/2-1.mp3?v=af91f9da", "dur": 240.51, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome to chapter two. Today we learn the actual language accountants write in: debits and credits. Stick with me, because once this clicks, everything else gets easier.", 0.0], ["First, why do businesses need a system at all? In chapter one, we updated the balance sheet after each transaction. Picture a real company doing that on a giant whiteboard, erasing and rewriting all day. Chaos. And afterward, no one could explain where the numbers came from. So every business needs a reliable accounting system, one that processes transactions into reports and keeps a record of every single one.", 11.55], ["The basic building block is the account. An account is a record for one item: one for Cash, one for Inventory, one for each kind of revenue and expense. All the accounts together are called the general ledger. Picture a notebook with a separate page for every account.", 41.72], ["Now, instead of saying an account went \"up\" or \"down,\" accountants say it was debited or credited. Debit simply means the left side. Credit means the right side. That's it. Left and right. Not good or bad.", 56.28], ["Why bother? Because of one powerful rule: in every correct entry, total debits equal total credits. So if they don't match, you know immediately that something's wrong. With plain pluses and minuses, a mistake can slip right by. Computer systems won't even accept an entry that doesn't balance.", 74.55], ["So which accounts go up with a debit, and which go up with a credit? Here's a memory trick. Two words: DEAD and RELIC.", 96.42], ["DEAD. Debits increase Expenses, Assets, and Dividends. D, E, A, D.", 104.12], ["RELIC. Revenues, Equity, and Liabilities Increase with Credits. R, E, L, I, C.", 107.39], ["Say them a few times. DEAD, RELIC. Honestly, it's like learning vocabulary in a new language. Memorize it first, and understanding follows fast.", 113.98], ["Let's try one. A customer pays ten thousand dollars they owed you. Which accounts? Cash and Accounts Receivable. Cash, an asset, goes up, so debit Cash. Accounts Receivable, also an asset, goes down, so credit it. Ten thousand on each side. Balanced.", 125.75], ["That's the two-step routine for every transaction. One: which accounts are affected, and are they going up or down? Two: apply the rules. What triggers the analysis? Usually a source document: a check, an invoice, a time card, a bank deposit slip. Good systems keep those documents and build in controls, like prenumbered forms and monthly bank reconciliations, so nothing slips through.", 143.02], ["To find an account's balance, start with the beginning balance and net out the debits and credits. Say Cash starts at fifty thousand, gets twenty-five thousand of debits and twelve thousand of credits. The balance is sixty-three thousand.", 175.25], ["Now let's bust two myths. Myth one: credits always mean decreases. That comes from stores saying \"we'll credit your account\" when you return something. But your account is a receivable on their books, an asset, so yes, a credit reduces it. Myth two: credits always mean increases. That comes from banks. When you deposit money, the bank owes it to you, so it's a liability for the bank, and liabilities increase with credits. The truth? Whether a credit increases or decreases depends entirely on the type of account.", 187.51], ["So, the takeaways. Each account tracks one item. Debits are left, credits are right, and they always balance. DEAD and RELIC tell you which way is up. Practice those, and you're speaking accounting. See you next lesson.", 224.41]]},
  "2-2": {"src": "talks/audio/2-2.mp3?v=ad2872b9", "dur": 221.73, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. Now that you know debits and credits, where do we actually write them down? In the journal.", 0.0], ["Think of a ship captain's logbook. Everything that happens gets written down, in order, by date. An accounting journal is the same idea. It's a chronological list of every transaction a company makes. But instead of long stories, each entry uses shorthand: which accounts are involved, and whether each one is debited or credited.", 6.83], ["The general journal is called the book of original entry. Here's why. A transaction happens, someone looks at the source document, figures out the debits and credits, and this is the very first place it gets recorded. You can't make financial statements from the journal alone, but it's where everything starts.", 28.51], ["Every entry follows the same layout. The date. Then the debited accounts, listed first and flush left. Then the credited accounts, indented to the right. And finally a short explanation of what happened. Debits first, credits indented, explanation last. Always.", 50.05], ["Let's watch a company called Xao Corporation in its first month. On day one, investors put money into the business in exchange for stock. Cash goes up, so we debit Cash. Equity goes up, so we credit Capital Stock. Next, Xao pays for some advertising. Advertising expense goes up, so it's debited. Cash goes down, so it's credited. See how DEAD and RELIC from last time do all the work?", 66.75], ["Now, a transaction doesn't have to involve just two accounts. On January twenty-eighth, Xao buys land for thirty thousand dollars. It pays five thousand in cash and signs a note promising to pay the other twenty-five thousand later. So the entry has one debit, Land for thirty thousand, and two credits: Cash for five thousand and Notes Payable for twenty-five thousand. Total debits, thirty thousand. Total credits, five plus twenty-five, also thirty thousand. That's called a compound entry. More accounts, same rule: debits equal credits.", 94.03], ["Real businesses can have thousands of transactions, many exactly alike. So some use special journals for the repetitive stuff. A cash receipts journal for every dollar coming in. A cash payments journal for every dollar going out. A sales journal for sales on credit, a purchases journal for purchases on credit, and a payroll journal for paychecks. The general journal is still used for everything else. Special journals just clear out the clutter.", 130.75], ["Another nice detail: journal pages are numbered. That creates a trail. If someone questions a number on the financial statements, you can trace it back to the exact page and the exact entry behind it. Some companies give every transaction its own index number for the same reason.", 160.06], ["But here's the limitation. Suppose I ask you: how much cash does Xao have at the end of January? With just a journal, you'd have to hunt through every page, find every debit and credit to Cash, and add them up. Tedious, and really easy to mess up. Now imagine hundreds of pages. We need a better tool, and that's the general ledger, coming up next.", 179.2], ["So, the recap. The journal is the book of original entry, every transaction in date order. Debits first, credits indented, and an explanation. Compound entries can have several accounts, but debits always equal credits. Great work. See you in the next one.", 204.57]]},
  "2-3": {"src": "talks/audio/2-3.mp3?v=20d1a4b4", "dur": 181.16, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. Last time we ended with a problem. The journal records everything in date order, but if you want to know how much cash a company has, you'd have to dig through every page. Today we fix that, with the general ledger.", 0.0], ["Picture two notebooks. The first one is the journal: transactions, one after another, by date. The second notebook is the ledger. It has one page for every account the company uses. A page for Cash. A page for Accounts Receivable. A page for Land, for Capital Stock, for each kind of revenue and expense. Xao Corporation used nine accounts in January, so its ledger has nine pages.", 15.7], ["So how do the amounts get from the first notebook to the second? That's called posting. To post means to copy each debit and credit from the journal onto the matching ledger page. Say the journal shows a debit to Cash and a credit to Capital Stock. You go to the Cash page and write the amount in the debit column. Then you go to the Capital Stock page and write it in the credit column. And each ledger page keeps a running balance, so after every posting you know exactly where that account stands.", 36.68], ["Now, two small habits make this system trustworthy. First, in the ledger, each line notes which journal page the amount came from. That keeps the ledger short, and it means anyone can trace any number back to the original entry. Second, in the journal, you put a check mark next to each entry once it's been posted. Why does that matter? Because in a manual system, it would be really easy to skip an entry, or post the same one twice. The check marks tell you exactly where you left off.", 73.02], ["Together, those cross-references create what's called an audit trail. You can start from a number on a financial statement, follow it into the ledger, then back to the journal, and all the way to the source document. Auditors love that. And honestly, so will you, the first time you need to find a mistake.", 103.54], ["So, remember last lesson, when we asked how much cash Xao had at the end of January? We had to hunt through the whole journal. Now, just flip to the Cash page in the ledger. There it is, twenty-six thousand three hundred dollars. Same answer, no hunting.", 121.38], ["Let's put the steps together so far. Step one: analyze each transaction. Which accounts are affected, and are they going up or down? Step two: journalize it, writing the debits and credits in the journal. Step three: periodically post those entries to the ledger. Analyze, journalize, post. That's the rhythm of accounting.", 140.55], ["So, quick recap. The general ledger has one page per account, each with a running balance. Posting copies journal amounts into the ledger. Page references and check marks create an audit trail, so nothing gets missed or doubled. And now we always know every account's balance. Nicely done. See you next time.", 161.37]]},
  "2-4": {"src": "talks/audio/2-4.mp3?v=4197bdc9", "dur": 213.79, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. So far we've analyzed transactions, written them in the journal, and posted them to the ledger. Today we do a quick self-check, called the trial balance.", 0.0], ["A trial balance is simply a list of every account in the ledger, with its balance. Accounts with debit balances, like assets and expenses, go in the debit column. Accounts with credit balances, like liabilities, equity, and revenue, go in the credit column. Then you add up both columns. Since every journal entry had equal debits and credits, the two totals should match. For Xao, both columns come to sixty-one thousand three hundred dollars. Balanced.", 11.25], ["Keep in mind, the trial balance isn't a financial statement. It's an internal check. If the totals don't match, you know there's an error somewhere, and you have to find it. Trust me, it's much easier to be careful as you go than to hunt for a mistake later.", 42.98], ["But here's the catch. A balanced trial balance does not prove everything is right. It only proves debits equal credits. Plenty of mistakes keep them equal. If you forget to record a transaction entirely, both sides are missing the same amount, so it still balances. If you record the same transaction twice, both sides get doubled, still balanced. And if you post an amount to the wrong account, say Rent Expense instead of Utilities Expense, the totals don't change at all. So a balanced trial balance is a good sign, but it's not a guarantee.", 59.3], ["The trial balance is also a handy springboard to the financial statements. Revenues and expenses go to the income statement. For Xao, revenue was eleven thousand and expenses were two thousand eight hundred, so net income was eight thousand two hundred. That flows into retained earnings, and the assets, liabilities, and equity go onto the balance sheet. In the next chapter, we'll add adjustments that make these statements fully up to date. For now, think: ledger, then trial balance, then statements.", 95.82], ["Next, let's get organized. A chart of accounts lists every account the company uses, usually with a number. It's a lot like zip codes for mail. A common pattern: accounts starting with one are assets, two are liabilities, three are equity, four are revenues, and five are expenses. Many systems let you type the number instead of the name. And numbers can be split further. If Accounts Receivable is one-oh-two, each customer could get their own sub-number.", 129.3], ["That leads to our last idea: control accounts and subsidiary accounts. Say customers owe a company nineteen thousand dollars in total, spread across three customers. The company needs the total, for the balance sheet, and it also needs to know what each customer owes, to send them bills. So it keeps a separate subsidiary account for each customer. The Accounts Receivable account in the general ledger is the control account, and its balance must always equal the total of all the customer accounts. Computers are great at keeping those in sync.", 161.15], ["So, to wrap up. The trial balance checks that debits equal credits, but it can't catch every error. Statements flow from it. A chart of accounts numbers everything. And a control account always equals the sum of its subsidiary accounts. Good work today.", 197.51]]},
  "2-5": {"src": "talks/audio/2-5.mp3?v=3cc29f88", "dur": 189.23, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. Let's step back and look at everything we've done this chapter, because there's an interesting pattern hiding in it.", 0.0], ["We analyzed a transaction and wrote the journal entry. Then we posted to the ledger, built a trial balance, and prepared financial statements. But think about which of those steps actually required thinking. Really, just the first one. Deciding which accounts are affected, and whether to debit or credit them, takes judgment. Everything after that is mechanical. Copying, adding, sorting, totaling.", 7.63], ["And mechanical work is exactly what computers are great at. That's why transaction processing was one of the very first business tasks ever computerized. Today, almost no one keeps their books in paper notebooks.", 34.32], ["The software comes in every size. A small business can buy a basic package for under a hundred dollars. A big organization might install an enterprise-wide system that costs millions, including the consultants to set it up. And more and more systems are cloud-based, where you enter data and run reports right over the internet.", 48.17], ["So what does using one look like? The data entry screen looks surprisingly familiar. There's a date, the accounts, debit and credit columns, and a memo line for the explanation. In other words, a journal entry. Once you save it, the software posts it to the ledger automatically, updates the trial balance, and refreshes the financial statements. And here's a nice safety feature: the software won't let you save an entry unless the debits equal the credits.", 70.87], ["Most accounting software shares a few common features. Simplified data entry screens. Separate modules for things like sales, purchases, payroll, and inventory. Pick lists and auto-complete, so you choose accounts instead of typing them. The ability to search and query the data. Information that's always up to date. And lots and lots of reports, at the push of a button.", 100.93], ["Now, you might be thinking: if the computer does all that, why am I learning debits and credits? Great question. Here's the answer, and it's an old saying in tech: garbage in, garbage out. The computer only handles the mechanical part. A person still decides what goes into that first journal entry. If that person doesn't understand accounts and debits and credits, they'll make bad choices at data entry, and every report the system produces will be wrong. Confidently, beautifully formatted, and wrong.", 123.27], ["So the knowledge you're building is what makes the software useful. You need it to set up a system, use it correctly, and make sense of the reports it spits out.", 157.0], ["So, the takeaways. After the first journal entry, accounting is mechanical, which is why computers took over. Software ranges from cheap small-business tools to huge enterprise and cloud systems. And no matter how good the software is, it needs someone who understands accounting at the keyboard. That's you. See you next time.", 168.81]]},
  "2-6": {"src": "talks/audio/2-6.mp3?v=a975e163", "dur": 155.09, "voice": "Jeremy", "paras": [["Welcome back. This is the last lesson of chapter two, and it's a fun one: a simple tool you'll use constantly, the T-account.", 0.0], ["Grab a piece of paper and draw a big capital letter T. Write an account name across the top. Now, the left side is for debits, and the right side is for credits. That's a T-account. That's the whole thing.", 8.02], ["It's not how real businesses keep their books. It's a scratch pad. Accountants sketch T-accounts to think through a few transactions quickly, or to explain something to a colleague, or, honestly, to figure out a tricky homework problem.", 24.15], ["To find the balance, add up each side. Whichever side is bigger, the balance is the difference, on that side.", 38.48], ["Let's try it with Xao Corporation's Cash account. On the left, all the debits, money coming in, total thirty-three thousand eight hundred dollars. On the right, all the credits, money going out, total seven thousand five hundred. The debits are bigger, so Cash has a debit balance of thirty-three thousand eight hundred minus seven thousand five hundred. That's twenty-six thousand three hundred. And look: it's exactly the same balance we found in the ledger account. Same information, just a faster, more visual layout.", 45.63], ["Here's why I love T-accounts. Lay out one for every account a company uses, and you can see a whole month on one page. Every debit, every credit, and every balance, at a glance. If you do that for Xao's January, the balances match the trial balance perfectly.", 79.86], ["A tip for practicing. When you get a transaction, sketch the T-accounts for the accounts involved. Put the debit on the left of one, the credit on the right of the other, and check that they're equal. And remember DEAD and RELIC from lesson one of this chapter: assets, expenses, and dividends grow on the left; revenues, equity, and liabilities grow on the right. With those two words and a few T's on scratch paper, you can solve a surprising number of accounting problems.", 98.2], ["So, the takeaways. A T-account has debits on the left and credits on the right. It's a thinking tool, not a bookkeeping system. And its balance is the bigger side minus the smaller side, which always matches the ledger. That wraps up chapter two. You now know how transactions travel from source document to journal, to ledger, to trial balance, to statements. That's a real accomplishment. Great work.", 129.03]]}
};
